WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.390
We know that motivation is very
important in language learning.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.560
It's a key part of that
learner's attitude.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.420
It goes along with confidence, you know,
the desire to achieve a certain level

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.160
in the language and motivation is what
gets us started and keeps us going.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.520
And I thought a bit about
motivation because it's easy to

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.940
talk about motivation, but how
do you motivate yourself or how

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.320
do you motivate other people?

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.790
What are the elements?

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.100
What are the components of motivation?

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.230
And I think there are five triggers to
motivation, and I want to talk about these

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:40.800
five triggers at three distinct stages
of our, uh, language learning journey.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.940
When we are getting started,
starting from scratch, obviously

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.620
a major trigger can be curiosity.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:54.690
People are interested in Spanish
culture or they've been watching Turkish

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:01.905
serial series on Netflix or anything
which triggers a curiosity in another

00:01:01.905 --> 00:01:04.035
language can be a major trigger.

00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:06.375
Another important trigger
can be a connection.

00:01:06.735 --> 00:01:10.875
For example, a family member, maybe
grandma who doesn't speak English

00:01:10.875 --> 00:01:14.235
for people who have immigrated,
say to an English speaking country.

00:01:14.655 --> 00:01:20.625
It can be, uh, a girlfriend, a boyfriend,
or simply a friend, or any kind of

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:24.915
a personal connection like that can
be a major, you know, contributor.

00:01:25.455 --> 00:01:30.195
A major trigger of interest in the
language, a major, major motivator.

00:01:30.195 --> 00:01:32.775
Another motivator can be an obligation.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.440
Maybe you work for a company,
say, in Japan, and they insist

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.970
on a certain level of English.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.259
So whether you like it or not,
you've gotta start learning English.

00:01:43.679 --> 00:01:47.820
Uh, at school certain
languages may be compulsory,

00:01:48.059 --> 00:01:50.639
so obligation can be a trigger.

00:01:50.669 --> 00:01:54.750
It can trigger resistance to the
language as well, but it does get you

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.210
going starting to learn the language.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:03.540
Now, uh, uh, on the other hand, a more
positive trigger is a sense of success.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:07.500
Let's say that you went to Mexico
and uh, you had a little phrase

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.120
book, and you were able to say a few
things and to communicate in Spanish.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.310
Then perhaps when you come home,
you're now more motivated to really

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:21.960
try to learn Spanish because you
had some success with the language.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:27.179
Success is, throughout the process,
a major trigger of motivation.

00:02:27.179 --> 00:02:28.260
And finally context.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:34.170
Again, getting back to the person who
visited Mexico or maybe visited China

00:02:34.174 --> 00:02:40.500
or Vietnam or any country and was made
aware that there is this other world

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.510
where people don't speak my native
language, they speak this other language.

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:50.700
And so there is a context where I can see
myself communicating in that language.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.864
So here is where context
becomes a trigger.

00:02:54.855 --> 00:02:59.475
Now, as we then approach the sort of
intermediate stage in the language,

00:03:00.045 --> 00:03:06.105
uh, we find that the same triggers
start to work as we've made sort

00:03:06.105 --> 00:03:07.904
of some progress in the language.

00:03:08.174 --> 00:03:12.435
We've gotten past the first
stage, we've acquired some words,

00:03:13.035 --> 00:03:17.595
and now we're discovering more
and more interesting content.

00:03:17.985 --> 00:03:20.475
In other words, our curiosity is growing.

00:03:20.535 --> 00:03:23.415
There's a tremendous opportunity
for that curiosity to grow.

00:03:23.595 --> 00:03:28.725
What was originally sort of a surface
like curiosity has now become a deeper

00:03:28.725 --> 00:03:31.485
curiosity in the intermediate stage.

00:03:31.995 --> 00:03:37.965
Similarly, with the connections, we can
now find that we were able to maybe start

00:03:37.965 --> 00:03:44.025
genuinely communicating with grandma
or on our most recent trip to Mexico,

00:03:44.025 --> 00:03:49.739
we were in fact able to communicate
more with the locals rather than just

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:51.960
asking for a beer in a, in a restaurant.

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:56.670
The idea of the connections is
strengthening and, and continuing

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.920
to, to trigger our motivation.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.750
So again, the obligation can get
stronger and reinforce itself.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:10.230
Perhaps you now have, after some
say, initial success in learning,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.320
say German, you feel you might
be able to go work in Germany.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:20.339
So you're building on your success in the
sort of initial and intermediate stage,

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.860
and you're adding additional obligations.

00:04:23.039 --> 00:04:25.770
Or maybe you say, I'm gonna take a test.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.690
I'm gonna take a test in
Korean, or a test in Chinese.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:35.880
And so you are prepared to put
obligations on yourself to keep motivating

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.010
yourself to go forward in the language.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.169
So obligation remains or
can remain a strong trigger.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.070
The next thing, of course, is success.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.620
So as we're in the intermediate stage,
we now have these eureka moments where

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.490
all of a sudden we understand this
passage or we're watching a movie and

00:04:53.495 --> 00:04:57.570
we understand the movie, or we were
with people and we were able to say

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.690
more things, uh, than previously.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.500
We kind of sensed some progress,
and that feeling of success

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.000
can be a tremendous trigger.

00:05:06.854 --> 00:05:09.585
In order to keep us motivated
to learn the language.

00:05:09.585 --> 00:05:12.375
Finally, again, the context is evolving.

00:05:12.705 --> 00:05:18.224
Like initially the context was
learner books and trying to get

00:05:18.224 --> 00:05:22.935
enough words to sort of pick your
way through, uh, the textbook.

00:05:23.445 --> 00:05:26.354
But now in the intermediate
stage, you're discovering

00:05:26.775 --> 00:05:30.015
YouTube videos series on Netflix.

00:05:30.224 --> 00:05:35.609
You may have started reading a book,
so you're creating a language context,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.609
which is motivating you to continue.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:44.489
So, and finally, when we reach
the sort of takeoff stage where we

00:05:44.489 --> 00:05:49.349
feel we're airborne, so to speak,
we have new triggers because the

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.870
curiosity trigger remains there.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.630
The more we get into the language,
the culture, meeting with

00:05:54.630 --> 00:06:00.075
people, wanting to travel, there
is no limit to our curiosity.

00:06:00.075 --> 00:06:05.115
And so curiosity continues to be a
very strong trigger of motivation.

00:06:05.115 --> 00:06:06.675
Again, connections.

00:06:06.885 --> 00:06:08.145
We make new friends.

00:06:08.415 --> 00:06:11.115
We have people that we regularly
have conversations with.

00:06:11.235 --> 00:06:14.535
It might be a tutor, it might
be a friend, it might be people

00:06:14.535 --> 00:06:16.095
we met visiting the country.

00:06:16.305 --> 00:06:21.505
But these connections only get stronger
and the connections continue to

00:06:21.525 --> 00:06:23.895
trigger our motivation in the language.

00:06:23.985 --> 00:06:29.660
Next is this obligation, which at various
stages of our learning can be a trigger,

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:32.060
even if at times, against our will.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.960
And now as we get airborne, as
I say, as we start to be more

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.950
comfortable in the language, we
get a bit of a perfectionist urge.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:41.690
And at least that has been my experience.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.600
I recognize that there are
shortcomings, there are grammatical

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.400
structures that I don't use correctly.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.280
I wanna work harder on those.

00:06:49.695 --> 00:06:54.344
Maybe that's where I get, um, uh, grammar
book written in the target language and

00:06:54.344 --> 00:07:00.344
focus in, again, in the target language
on trying to refine my, uh, control

00:07:00.344 --> 00:07:04.815
of the language as well as wanting
to read more and more and listen more

00:07:04.815 --> 00:07:07.245
and more to increase my vocabulary.

00:07:08.054 --> 00:07:11.265
Uh, you know, on LingQ I
talk about getting over 40...

00:07:11.354 --> 00:07:15.420
it depends on the language, but
over 40 or 50,000 known words.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:16.410
I wanna push it.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.670
Uh, in Russian, I'm at
90,000 known words in check.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.830
Now granted, Slavic languages are very
heavily inflected and we count every

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.870
form of the word as a new word on LingQ.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:34.410
So I can't go bragging that I know all
these words, but it is a motivator, this

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:39.750
attempt to try and push my vocabulary
limit, uh, increase my accuracy in the

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.480
language we get that perfectionist urge.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.740
Once we have the feeling that we're
sort of airborne in the language.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.880
Similarly, when it comes to the
sense of success, we now find that

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.780
lo and behold, we don't have to
translate everything from English.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:02.000
There may still be moments when we're
in our minds translating from, or

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:05.610
whatever your native language is,
translating from your native language,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.800
but increasingly, We're just speaking.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.690
We understand, we respond.

00:08:09.719 --> 00:08:13.380
We speak in the language,
our thought processes, the

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.880
way we organize our thoughts.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.219
Everything now is falling into place.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:22.740
We are doing it in the way that
language does it increasingly, and doing

00:08:22.745 --> 00:08:27.420
that, that sense of success of being
airborne in the language is in itself a

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.180
trigger and encourages us to continue.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:34.380
And finally talking about context.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:40.140
All of a sudden we're in a situation
where the language triggers the language.

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:48.690
We are no longer, you know, looking for
cues that, you know, help us understand.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.240
Like someone says something in
the language, what did he say?

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.300
We are responding to the language.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:09:00.660
We have that context, that language
context, so firmly in our, in our brain

00:09:01.275 --> 00:09:06.645
that we are able to respond almost
without thinking in the language.

00:09:06.825 --> 00:09:12.195
So we are totally sort of embedded in
the language context and that feeling

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:14.835
also is tremendously motivating.

00:09:15.135 --> 00:09:17.505
So I hope you found this useful.

00:09:17.655 --> 00:09:19.665
Uh, we need to be motivated.

00:09:19.665 --> 00:09:23.895
There are different things that
trigger our motivation at different

00:09:24.165 --> 00:09:25.665
stages in our language learning.

00:09:25.995 --> 00:09:30.405
But it's, it's very important to our
success to be open to these triggers

00:09:30.615 --> 00:09:36.885
in order to remain motivated and move
along, ultimately to, uh, I guess people

00:09:36.885 --> 00:09:41.715
typically wanna get to a B2 level or a, a
comfortable fluency level in the language.

00:09:41.925 --> 00:09:46.815
And I think everyone is capable of doing
that as long as they remain motivated.

00:09:47.325 --> 00:09:48.165
Thank you for listening.

00:09:48.345 --> 00:09:48.855
Bye for now.

